module

public import Mathlib.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.ChangeOfRings
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Quotient.Basic
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.TensorProduct.Tower

/-!
# Extension of scalars along a homomorphism of not necessarily commutative rings

Mathlib's `ModuleCat.extendScalars` is stated for **commutative** rings, because it is
`S ⊗[R] M` and `TensorProduct` needs a `CommSemiring` base. This file constructs the left
adjoint of `ModuleCat.restrictScalars f` for an arbitrary ring homomorphism `f : A →+* B`
between arbitrary (not necessarily commutative) rings, by building the relative tensor
product `B ⊗[A] M` directly as a quotient of `B ⊗[ℤ] M`:

`B ⊗[A] M := (B ⊗[ℤ] M) ⧸ span B {(b * f a) ⊗ m - b ⊗ (a • m)}`.

Here `B ⊗[ℤ] M` is already a `B`-module through `TensorProduct.leftModule`, and the span of
any set is a `B`-submodule, so the quotient is a `B`-module with no further work. Its
universal property is `TensorProduct.AlgebraTensorModule.lift` — which, unlike
`TensorProduct.lift`, allows the left-hand ring to be noncommutative — followed by
`Submodule.liftQ`.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.NoncommChangeOfRings.tensorRel f M`: the `B`-submodule of `B ⊗[ℤ] M` generated by
  the relations `(b * f a) ⊗ m - b ⊗ (a • m)`.
* `Stacks.NoncommChangeOfRings.BaseChange f M`: the relative tensor product `B ⊗[A] M`, as a
  `B`-module, with `tmul f b m` its pure tensor and `ι f : M →ₛₗ[f] B ⊗[A] M` the unit
  `m ↦ 1 ⊗ m`.
* `Stacks.NoncommChangeOfRings.desc f ψ`: the `B`-linear map `B ⊗[A] M →ₗ[B] N` determined by
  an `f`-semilinear map `ψ : M →ₛₗ[f] N`.
* `Stacks.NoncommChangeOfRings.squareMap f₁ f₂ hcomm φ`: base change of a semilinear map
  along a commuting square `σ ∘ f₁ = f₂ ∘ ρ` of ring homomorphisms, the `σ`-semilinear map
  `B₁ ⊗[A₁] M₁ →ₛₗ[σ] B₂ ⊗[A₂] M₂` sending `b ⊗ m` to `σ b ⊗ φ m`, and
  `squareMapObj` the same map read between the `ModuleCat` objects. This is what the
  restriction maps of a presheaf of modules are built from.
* `Stacks.NoncommChangeOfRings.map f ψ`: functoriality of `B ⊗[A] -` in the `A`-module, the
  special case of `squareMap` for the square with identity vertical edges.
* `Stacks.NoncommChangeOfRings.extendScalars f : ModuleCat A ⥤ ModuleCat B`: extension of
  scalars along `f`, with no commutativity hypothesis on `A` or `B`, together with
  `objTmul f b m` for its pure tensors and `ofRestrict f g` for the semilinear map
  underlying a morphism into a restriction of scalars.

## Main results

* `Stacks.NoncommChangeOfRings.hom_ext`: two semilinear maps out of `B ⊗[A] M` agree as soon
  as they agree on the pure tensors `1 ⊗ m`; `extendScalars_hom_ext`, `restrict_hom_ext` and
  `restrictScalars_hom_ext` are its `ModuleCat` forms.
* `Stacks.NoncommChangeOfRings.descEquiv`: the universal property of the relative tensor
  product, `(B ⊗[A] M →ₗ[B] N) ≃ (M →ₛₗ[f] N)`.
* `Stacks.NoncommChangeOfRings.extendRestrictScalarsAdj`: `extendScalars f` is left adjoint
  to `ModuleCat.restrictScalars f`, for an arbitrary ring homomorphism `f : A →+* B`. The
  instances `(extendScalars f).IsLeftAdjoint` and
  `(ModuleCat.restrictScalars f).IsRightAdjoint` are registered; Mathlib provides the latter
  only for commutative rings.

## Implementation notes

**Why `ℤ`.** `ModuleCat` objects are `AddCommGroup`s, so `Module ℤ M` is available for free
via `AddCommGroup.toIntModule` and there is exactly one such instance for every module in
sight. That matters: the reason `ModuleCat.extendScalars` is painful to use is that
`(ModuleCat.extendScalars f).obj M` carries `B ⊗[A] M` for the `Module A B` instance that
`ModuleCat.restrictScalars f` installs, which is definitionally but not syntactically
`Module.compHom B f`, so pure-tensor rewrite rules silently fail to fire. Taking the base to
be `ℤ` removes that choice entirely — `Module ℤ B` and `Module ℤ M` have a single instance
path each — and so no proof below needs a transparency workaround.

**One head symbol per spelling.** `tmul f b m` is a `def`, not an abbreviation for
`b ⊗ₜ[ℤ] m` followed by the quotient map, and no rewrite lemma below has a bare `⊗ₜ` in its
statement. Its `ModuleCat` counterpart `objTmul` exists because `↑((extendScalars f).obj M)`
and `BaseChange f ↑M` are definitionally but not syntactically equal, and a goal mixing them
is reported by `rw` as "not type-correct under the `implicit` transparency level", after
which every further rewrite fails; `ofRestrict` plays the same role for
`↑((ModuleCat.restrictScalars σ).obj N)` against `↑N`. For the same reason `map_tmul`,
`squareMap_tmul`, `extendScalars_map_objTmul` and `ofRestrict_squareMapObj` are all arranged
to hold by `rfl`: `squareMap` is built from `TensorProduct.liftAddHom` rather than from
`desc`, which would introduce a spurious `b * 1`, and `map` is `squareMap` at an identity
square rather than `desc` of `ι ∘ ψ`. This is the discipline `Stacks.PullbackModel.tmul`
adopts for the commutative case.

**Universes.** `A : Type u₁` and `B : Type u₂` are independent, and the module universe is
`max u₂ v`: an `A`-module in `Type (max u₂ v)` has `B ⊗[ℤ] M` in `Type (max u₂ v)` as well,
so the functor is an endo-universe one and composes with `ModuleCat.restrictScalars`, which
preserves the module universe. This is exactly the universe shape of Mathlib's
`ModuleCat.extendRestrictScalarsAdj`, namely
`extendScalars.{u₁, u₂, max v u₂} f ⊣ restrictScalars.{max v u₂, u₁, u₂} f`; nothing is
narrowed relative to it, and the commutativity hypotheses are dropped. In `squareMap` the
four rings have four independent universes; `squareMapObj` needs the two module universes to
agree and so puts the two source rings in one universe and the two target rings in another.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 03CZ](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03CZ)
-/

@[expose] public section

open CategoryTheory TensorProduct

universe v w u₁ u₂ u₃ u₄

namespace Stacks.NoncommChangeOfRings

variable {A : Type u₁} {B : Type u₂} [Ring A] [Ring B] (f : A →+* B)

/-! ### The relative tensor product -/

section Relation

variable (M : Type v) [AddCommGroup M] [Module A M]

/-- The `B`-submodule of `B ⊗[ℤ] M` generated by the elements `(b * f a) ⊗ m - b ⊗ (a • m)`,
whose quotient is the relative tensor product `B ⊗[A] M` along `f : A →+* B`. -/
def tensorRel : Submodule B (B ⊗[ℤ] M) :=
  Submodule.span B
    {x | ∃ (b : B) (a : A) (m : M), x = (b * f a) ⊗ₜ[ℤ] m - b ⊗ₜ[ℤ] (a • m)}

/-- The relative tensor product `B ⊗[A] M` of an `A`-module `M` along a homomorphism
`f : A →+* B` of not necessarily commutative rings: the quotient of `B ⊗[ℤ] M` by the
relations `(b * f a) ⊗ m = b ⊗ (a • m)`. -/
def BaseChange : Type max u₂ v := (B ⊗[ℤ] M) ⧸ tensorRel f M

/-- The additive group structure on `B ⊗[A] M`, inherited from the quotient. -/
instance : AddCommGroup (BaseChange f M) :=
  inferInstanceAs (AddCommGroup ((B ⊗[ℤ] M) ⧸ tensorRel f M))

/-- The `B`-module structure on `B ⊗[A] M`, inherited from the quotient of the `B`-module
`B ⊗[ℤ] M`. -/
instance : Module B (BaseChange f M) :=
  inferInstanceAs (Module B ((B ⊗[ℤ] M) ⧸ tensorRel f M))

/-- The quotient map `B ⊗[ℤ] M → B ⊗[A] M`. -/
def mk : (B ⊗[ℤ] M) →ₗ[B] BaseChange f M := (tensorRel f M).mkQ

/-- The quotient map `B ⊗[ℤ] M → B ⊗[A] M` is surjective. -/
lemma mk_surjective : Function.Surjective (mk f M) := Submodule.mkQ_surjective _

/-- The quotient map `B ⊗[ℤ] M → B ⊗[A] M` kills the relations `tensorRel f M`. -/
lemma mk_eq_zero_of_mem {x : B ⊗[ℤ] M} (hx : x ∈ tensorRel f M) : mk f M x = 0 :=
  (Submodule.Quotient.mk_eq_zero _).2 hx

end Relation

/-! ### Pure tensors -/

section Tmul

variable {M : Type v} [AddCommGroup M] [Module A M]

/-- The pure tensor `b ⊗ m` in the relative tensor product `B ⊗[A] M`. -/
def tmul (b : B) (m : M) : BaseChange f M := mk f M (b ⊗ₜ[ℤ] m)

/-- Scalar multiplication by `b` multiplies the left factor of a pure tensor by `b`. -/
@[simp]
lemma smul_tmul (b b' : B) (m : M) : b • tmul f b' m = tmul f (b * b') m := rfl

/-- Every pure tensor `b ⊗ m` is `b • (1 ⊗ m)`. -/
lemma tmul_eq_smul_tmul_one (b : B) (m : M) : tmul f b m = b • tmul f 1 m := by
  rw [smul_tmul, mul_one]

/-- A pure tensor is additive in its right factor. -/
lemma tmul_add (b : B) (m m' : M) : tmul f b (m + m') = tmul f b m + tmul f b m' := by
  simp only [tmul, TensorProduct.tmul_add, map_add]

/-- A pure tensor is additive in its left factor. -/
lemma add_tmul (b b' : B) (m : M) : tmul f (b + b') m = tmul f b m + tmul f b' m := by
  simp only [tmul, TensorProduct.add_tmul, map_add]

/-- A pure tensor with right factor `0` is `0`. -/
@[simp]
lemma tmul_zero (b : B) : tmul f b (0 : M) = 0 := by
  simp only [tmul, TensorProduct.tmul_zero, map_zero]

/-- A pure tensor with left factor `0` is `0`. -/
@[simp]
lemma zero_tmul (m : M) : tmul f (0 : B) m = 0 := by
  simp only [tmul, TensorProduct.zero_tmul, map_zero]

/-- The defining relation of the relative tensor product: a scalar of `A` may be moved across
the tensor sign, after being pushed into `B` along `f`. -/
lemma tmul_smul (b : B) (a : A) (m : M) : tmul f (b * f a) m = tmul f b (a • m) := by
  rw [← sub_eq_zero, tmul, tmul, ← map_sub]
  exact mk_eq_zero_of_mem f M (Submodule.subset_span ⟨b, a, m, rfl⟩)

/-- Every element of `B ⊗[A] M` is built from pure tensors. -/
lemma induction_on {motive : BaseChange f M → Prop} (x : BaseChange f M) (zero : motive 0)
    (pure : ∀ (b : B) (m : M), motive (tmul f b m))
    (add : ∀ x y, motive x → motive y → motive (x + y)) : motive x := by
  obtain ⟨y, rfl⟩ := mk_surjective f M x
  induction y using TensorProduct.induction_on with
  | zero => simpa only [map_zero] using zero
  | tmul b m => exact pure b m
  | add y z hy hz => simpa only [map_add] using add _ _ hy hz

/-- Two semilinear maps out of `B ⊗[A] M` agree as soon as they agree on the pure tensors
`1 ⊗ m`. Taking `σ` to be `RingHom.id B` this is the statement for `B`-linear maps. -/
lemma hom_ext {B' : Type u₃} [Ring B'] {σ : B →+* B'} {N : Type w} [AddCommGroup N]
    [Module B' N] {g h : BaseChange f M →ₛₗ[σ] N}
    (H : ∀ m : M, g (tmul f 1 m) = h (tmul f 1 m)) : g = h := by
  refine LinearMap.ext fun x =>
    induction_on f (motive := fun x => g x = h x) x ?_ (fun b m => ?_) (fun y z hy hz => ?_)
  · show g 0 = h 0
    rw [map_zero, map_zero]
  · show g (tmul f b m) = h (tmul f b m)
    rw [tmul_eq_smul_tmul_one, map_smulₛₗ, map_smulₛₗ, H]
  · show g (y + z) = h (y + z)
    rw [map_add, map_add, hy, hz]

/-- The unit of the adjunction, `m ↦ 1 ⊗ m`, as an `f`-semilinear map. -/
def ι : M →ₛₗ[f] BaseChange f M where
  toFun m := tmul f 1 m
  map_add' := tmul_add f 1
  map_smul' a m := by rw [smul_tmul, mul_one, ← tmul_smul, one_mul]

/-- The unit `ι f` sends `m` to `1 ⊗ m`. -/
@[simp]
lemma ι_apply (m : M) : ι f m = tmul f 1 m := rfl

end Tmul

/-! ### The universal property -/

section Desc

variable {M : Type v} [AddCommGroup M] [Module A M] {N : Type w} [AddCommGroup N] [Module B N]

/-- The `B`-linear map `B ⊗[A] M →ₗ[B] N` determined by an `f`-semilinear map
`ψ : M →ₛₗ[f] N`, sending `b ⊗ m` to `b • ψ m`. -/
def desc (ψ : M →ₛₗ[f] N) : BaseChange f M →ₗ[B] N :=
  Submodule.liftQ _
    (AlgebraTensorModule.lift
      (LinearMap.toSpanSingleton B (M →ₗ[ℤ] N) ψ.toAddMonoidHom.toIntLinearMap))
    (by
      have key : ∀ (b : B) (m : M),
          AlgebraTensorModule.lift
            (LinearMap.toSpanSingleton B (M →ₗ[ℤ] N) ψ.toAddMonoidHom.toIntLinearMap)
              (b ⊗ₜ[ℤ] m) = b • ψ m := fun _ _ => rfl
      rw [tensorRel, Submodule.span_le]
      rintro x ⟨b, a, m, rfl⟩
      simp only [SetLike.mem_coe, LinearMap.mem_ker, map_sub, key, map_smulₛₗ, smul_smul,
        sub_self])

/-- `desc f ψ` sends `b ⊗ m` to `b • ψ m`. -/
@[simp]
lemma desc_tmul (ψ : M →ₛₗ[f] N) (b : B) (m : M) : desc f ψ (tmul f b m) = b • ψ m := rfl

/-- `desc f ψ` restricts to `ψ` along the unit `ι f`. -/
@[simp]
lemma desc_ι (ψ : M →ₛₗ[f] N) (m : M) : desc f ψ (ι f m) = ψ m := by
  rw [ι_apply, desc_tmul, one_smul]

/-- **The universal property of the relative tensor product.** Composing with the unit
`ι f : M →ₛₗ[f] B ⊗[A] M` is a bijection from the `B`-linear maps out of `B ⊗[A] M` to the
`f`-semilinear maps out of `M`, with inverse `desc f`. -/
def descEquiv : (BaseChange f M →ₗ[B] N) ≃ (M →ₛₗ[f] N) where
  toFun g := g.comp (ι f)
  invFun := desc f
  left_inv _ := hom_ext f fun m => by simp only [desc_tmul, one_smul, LinearMap.comp_apply,
    ι_apply]
  right_inv ψ := LinearMap.ext fun m => by simp only [LinearMap.comp_apply, desc_ι]

/-- `descEquiv f g` sends `m` to `g (1 ⊗ m)`. -/
@[simp]
lemma descEquiv_apply (g : BaseChange f M →ₗ[B] N) (m : M) :
    descEquiv f g m = g (tmul f 1 m) := rfl

/-- The inverse of `descEquiv f` is `desc f`. -/
@[simp]
lemma descEquiv_symm_apply (ψ : M →ₛₗ[f] N) : (descEquiv f).symm ψ = desc f ψ := rfl

end Desc

/-! ### Base change along a commuting square of ring homomorphisms -/

section Square

variable {A₁ : Type u₁} {B₁ : Type u₂} {A₂ : Type u₃} {B₂ : Type u₄}
  [Ring A₁] [Ring B₁] [Ring A₂] [Ring B₂]
  (f₁ : A₁ →+* B₁) (f₂ : A₂ →+* B₂) {ρ : A₁ →+* A₂} {σ : B₁ →+* B₂}
  (hcomm : ∀ a, σ (f₁ a) = f₂ (ρ a))
  {M₁ : Type v} [AddCommGroup M₁] [Module A₁ M₁]
  {M₂ : Type w} [AddCommGroup M₂] [Module A₂ M₂]

/-- `b ⊗ m ↦ σ b ⊗ φ m`, as an additive map out of `B₁ ⊗[ℤ] M₁`. -/
def squareAddHom (φ : M₁ →ₛₗ[ρ] M₂) : (B₁ ⊗[ℤ] M₁) →+ BaseChange f₂ M₂ :=
  TensorProduct.liftAddHom
    (AddMonoidHom.mk'
      (fun b => AddMonoidHom.mk' (fun m => tmul f₂ (σ b) (φ m)) fun x y => by
        rw [map_add, tmul_add])
      fun x y => by
        ext m
        simp only [AddMonoidHom.mk'_apply, AddMonoidHom.add_apply, map_add, add_tmul])
    fun r b m => by
      change mk f₂ M₂ ((σ (r • b)) ⊗ₜ[ℤ] (φ m)) = mk f₂ M₂ ((σ b) ⊗ₜ[ℤ] (φ (r • m)))
      rw [map_zsmul σ r b, map_zsmul φ r m, TensorProduct.smul_tmul]

/-- `b ⊗ m ↦ σ b ⊗ φ m`, as a `σ`-semilinear map out of `B₁ ⊗[ℤ] M₁`. -/
def squareTensorₛₗ (φ : M₁ →ₛₗ[ρ] M₂) : (B₁ ⊗[ℤ] M₁) →ₛₗ[σ] BaseChange f₂ M₂ where
  toFun := squareAddHom (σ := σ) f₂ φ
  map_add' := map_add _
  map_smul' b x := by
    induction x using TensorProduct.induction_on with
    | zero => rw [smul_zero, map_zero, smul_zero]
    | tmul b' m =>
        change tmul f₂ (σ (b * b')) (φ m) = σ b • tmul f₂ (σ b') (φ m)
        rw [map_mul, smul_tmul]
    | add x y hx hy => rw [smul_add, map_add, map_add, hx, hy, smul_add]

include hcomm in
/-- Base change of a semilinear map `φ : M₁ →ₛₗ[ρ] M₂` along a commuting square of ring
homomorphisms: the `σ`-semilinear map `B₁ ⊗[A₁] M₁ →ₛₗ[σ] B₂ ⊗[A₂] M₂` sending `b ⊗ m` to
`σ b ⊗ φ m`. -/
def squareMap (φ : M₁ →ₛₗ[ρ] M₂) : BaseChange f₁ M₁ →ₛₗ[σ] BaseChange f₂ M₂ :=
  Submodule.liftQ _ (squareTensorₛₗ (σ := σ) f₂ φ) (by
    have key : ∀ (b : B₁) (m : M₁),
        squareTensorₛₗ (σ := σ) f₂ φ (b ⊗ₜ[ℤ] m) = tmul f₂ (σ b) (φ m) := fun _ _ => rfl
    rw [tensorRel, Submodule.span_le]
    rintro x ⟨b, a, m, rfl⟩
    simp only [SetLike.mem_coe, LinearMap.mem_ker, map_sub, key, map_mul, hcomm, map_smulₛₗ,
      ← tmul_smul, sub_self])

/-- `squareMap f₁ f₂ hcomm φ` sends `b ⊗ m` to `σ b ⊗ φ m`. -/
@[simp]
lemma squareMap_tmul (φ : M₁ →ₛₗ[ρ] M₂) (b : B₁) (m : M₁) :
    squareMap f₁ f₂ hcomm φ (tmul f₁ b m) = tmul f₂ (σ b) (φ m) := rfl

end Square

/-! ### Functoriality -/

section Map

variable {M M' M'' : Type v} [AddCommGroup M] [Module A M] [AddCommGroup M'] [Module A M']
  [AddCommGroup M''] [Module A M'']

/-- Base change of an `A`-linear map: the `B`-linear map `B ⊗[A] M →ₗ[B] B ⊗[A] M'` sending
`b ⊗ m` to `b ⊗ ψ m`. This is `squareMap` for the square with both horizontal edges `f` and
both vertical edges an identity, which is what makes `map_tmul` hold by `rfl`. -/
def map (ψ : M →ₗ[A] M') : BaseChange f M →ₗ[B] BaseChange f M' :=
  squareMap (σ := RingHom.id B) f f (fun _ => rfl) ψ

/-- `map f ψ` sends `b ⊗ m` to `b ⊗ ψ m`. -/
@[simp]
lemma map_tmul (ψ : M →ₗ[A] M') (b : B) (m : M) :
    map f ψ (tmul f b m) = tmul f b (ψ m) := rfl

/-- Base change of the identity is the identity. -/
lemma map_id : map f (LinearMap.id : M →ₗ[A] M) = LinearMap.id :=
  hom_ext f fun m => by simp only [map_tmul, LinearMap.id_coe, id_eq]

/-- Base change of a composite is the composite of the base changes. -/
lemma map_comp (ψ : M →ₗ[A] M') (χ : M' →ₗ[A] M'') :
    map f (χ.comp ψ) = (map f χ).comp (map f ψ) :=
  hom_ext f fun m => by simp only [map_tmul, LinearMap.comp_apply]

end Map

/-! ### Extension of scalars as a functor, and the adjunction -/

section Functor

/-- **Extension of scalars along a homomorphism of not necessarily commutative rings**: the
functor `M ↦ B ⊗[A] M`, `ψ ↦ 1 ⊗ ψ`. This is `ModuleCat.extendScalars` with the two
commutativity hypotheses removed. -/
def extendScalars : ModuleCat.{max u₂ v} A ⥤ ModuleCat.{max u₂ v} B where
  obj M := ModuleCat.of B (BaseChange f M)
  map ψ := ModuleCat.ofHom (map f ψ.hom)
  map_id _ := ModuleCat.hom_ext (map_id f)
  map_comp ψ χ := ModuleCat.hom_ext (map_comp f ψ.hom χ.hom)

/-- The pure tensor `b ⊗ m` as an element of `(extendScalars f).obj M`. The underlying type of
that object is `BaseChange f M` only after unfolding the functor, so a pure tensor written
with `tmul` is of a syntactically distinct type and poisons every subsequent rewrite; every
statement at the `ModuleCat` level is phrased through this abbreviation instead. See the
implementation notes. -/
def objTmul {M : ModuleCat.{max u₂ v} A} (b : B) (m : M) : (extendScalars f).obj M :=
  tmul f b m

/-- `(extendScalars f).map ψ` sends `b ⊗ m` to `b ⊗ ψ m`. -/
@[simp]
lemma extendScalars_map_objTmul {M N : ModuleCat.{max u₂ v} A} (ψ : M ⟶ N) (b : B) (m : M) :
    (extendScalars f).map ψ (objTmul f b m) = objTmul f b (ψ m) := rfl

/-- The `f`-semilinear map `M →ₛₗ[f] N` underlying a morphism
`M ⟶ (ModuleCat.restrictScalars f).obj N`. Applying such a morphism to an element produces a
term of the syntactically distinct type `↑((ModuleCat.restrictScalars f).obj N)`, so every
computation below is phrased through this map instead; see the implementation notes. -/
noncomputable def ofRestrict {M : ModuleCat.{w} A} {N : ModuleCat.{w} B}
    (g : M ⟶ (ModuleCat.restrictScalars f).obj N) : ↑M →ₛₗ[f] ↑N :=
  (ModuleCat.semilinearMapAddEquiv f M N).symm g

/-- Two morphisms into a restriction of scalars agree as soon as their underlying semilinear
maps do. -/
lemma restrictScalars_hom_ext {M : ModuleCat.{w} A} {N : ModuleCat.{w} B}
    {x y : M ⟶ (ModuleCat.restrictScalars f).obj N}
    (H : ∀ m : M, ofRestrict f x m = ofRestrict f y m) : x = y :=
  (ModuleCat.semilinearMapAddEquiv f M N).symm.injective (LinearMap.ext H)

/-- The adjunction bijection `Hom_B(B ⊗[A] M, N) ≃ Hom_A(M, N)`, read between the `ModuleCat`
hom-types through `ModuleCat.semilinearMapAddEquiv`. -/
noncomputable def homEquiv (M : ModuleCat.{max u₂ v} A) (N : ModuleCat.{max u₂ v} B) :
    ((extendScalars f).obj M ⟶ N) ≃ (M ⟶ (ModuleCat.restrictScalars f).obj N) :=
  ModuleCat.homEquiv.trans
    ((descEquiv f).trans (ModuleCat.semilinearMapAddEquiv f M N).toEquiv)

/-- The semilinear map underlying `homEquiv f M N g` sends `m` to `g (1 ⊗ m)`. -/
@[simp]
lemma ofRestrict_homEquiv {M : ModuleCat.{max u₂ v} A} {N : ModuleCat.{max u₂ v} B}
    (g : (extendScalars f).obj M ⟶ N) (m : M) :
    ofRestrict f (homEquiv f M N g) m = g (objTmul f 1 m) := rfl

/-- The inverse of `homEquiv f M N` sends `g` to the map `b ⊗ m ↦ b • g m`. -/
@[simp]
lemma homEquiv_symm_apply_objTmul {M : ModuleCat.{max u₂ v} A} {N : ModuleCat.{max u₂ v} B}
    (g : M ⟶ (ModuleCat.restrictScalars f).obj N) (b : B) (m : M) :
    (homEquiv f M N).symm g (objTmul f b m) = b • ofRestrict f g m := rfl

/-- Two morphisms out of `B ⊗[A] M` in `ModuleCat B` agree as soon as they agree on the pure
tensors `1 ⊗ m`. -/
lemma extendScalars_hom_ext {M : ModuleCat.{max u₂ v} A} {N : ModuleCat.{max u₂ v} B}
    {x y : (extendScalars f).obj M ⟶ N} (H : ∀ m : M, x (objTmul f 1 m) = y (objTmul f 1 m)) :
    x = y :=
  ModuleCat.hom_ext (hom_ext f H)

/-- Two morphisms from `B ⊗[A] M` into a restriction of scalars along `σ : B →+* B'` agree as
soon as their underlying `σ`-semilinear maps agree on the pure tensors `1 ⊗ m`. This is the
shape in which the restriction maps of a presheaf of modules are compared. -/
lemma restrict_hom_ext {B' : Type u₃} [Ring B'] (σ : B →+* B')
    {M : ModuleCat.{max u₂ v} A} {N : ModuleCat.{max u₂ v} B'}
    {x y : (extendScalars f).obj M ⟶ (ModuleCat.restrictScalars σ).obj N}
    (H : ∀ m : M, ofRestrict σ x (objTmul f 1 m) = ofRestrict σ y (objTmul f 1 m)) : x = y :=
  restrictScalars_hom_ext σ fun _ => LinearMap.congr_fun (hom_ext f H) _

/-- Naturality of the adjunction bijection in the `A`-module. -/
lemma homEquiv_symm_naturality_left {M' M : ModuleCat.{max u₂ v} A}
    {N : ModuleCat.{max u₂ v} B} (ψ : M' ⟶ M)
    (g : M ⟶ (ModuleCat.restrictScalars f).obj N) :
    (homEquiv f M' N).symm (ψ ≫ g) =
      (extendScalars f).map ψ ≫ (homEquiv f M N).symm g :=
  extendScalars_hom_ext f fun _ => by
    rw [homEquiv_symm_apply_objTmul, ModuleCat.comp_apply, extendScalars_map_objTmul,
      homEquiv_symm_apply_objTmul, one_smul, one_smul]
    rfl

/-- Naturality of the adjunction bijection in the `B`-module. -/
lemma homEquiv_naturality_right {M : ModuleCat.{max u₂ v} A} {N N' : ModuleCat.{max u₂ v} B}
    (x : (extendScalars f).obj M ⟶ N) (y : N ⟶ N') :
    homEquiv f M N' (x ≫ y) =
      homEquiv f M N x ≫ (ModuleCat.restrictScalars f).map y := rfl

/-- **Extension of scalars is left adjoint to restriction of scalars**, for an arbitrary ring
homomorphism `f : A →+* B` between not necessarily commutative rings. Concretely, for an
`A`-module `M` and a `B`-module `N` there is a bijection
`Hom_B(B ⊗[A] M, N) = Hom_A(M, N)`, natural in `M` and `N`. -/
noncomputable def extendRestrictScalarsAdj :
    extendScalars.{v} f ⊣ ModuleCat.restrictScalars.{max u₂ v} f :=
  Adjunction.mkOfHomEquiv
    { homEquiv := homEquiv f
      homEquiv_naturality_left_symm := fun {_ _ _} ψ g =>
        homEquiv_symm_naturality_left f ψ g
      homEquiv_naturality_right := fun {_ _ _} x y => homEquiv_naturality_right f x y }

/-- Extension of scalars along `f` is a left adjoint. -/
instance isLeftAdjoint_extendScalars : (extendScalars.{v} f).IsLeftAdjoint :=
  (extendRestrictScalarsAdj.{v} f).isLeftAdjoint

/-- Restriction of scalars along an arbitrary ring homomorphism `f` is a right adjoint. -/
instance isRightAdjoint_restrictScalars :
    (ModuleCat.restrictScalars.{max u₂ v} f).IsRightAdjoint :=
  (extendRestrictScalarsAdj.{v} f).isRightAdjoint

/-- The unit of `extendRestrictScalarsAdj f` at `M` sends `m` to `1 ⊗ m`. -/
@[simp]
lemma extendRestrictScalarsAdj_unit_app_apply (M : ModuleCat.{max u₂ v} A) (m : M) :
    ofRestrict f ((extendRestrictScalarsAdj.{v} f).unit.app M) m = objTmul f 1 m := rfl

/-- The counit of `extendRestrictScalarsAdj f` at `N` sends `b ⊗ n` to `b • n`. -/
@[simp]
lemma extendRestrictScalarsAdj_counit_app_apply_objTmul (N : ModuleCat.{max u₂ v} B) (b : B)
    (n : N) :
    (extendRestrictScalarsAdj.{v} f).counit.app N (objTmul f b n) = b • n := rfl

end Functor

/-! ### Base change along a commuting square, at the level of `ModuleCat` -/

section SquareObj

variable {A₁ A₂ : Type u₁} {B₁ B₂ : Type u₂} [Ring A₁] [Ring B₁] [Ring A₂] [Ring B₂]
  (f₁ : A₁ →+* B₁) (f₂ : A₂ →+* B₂) {ρ : A₁ →+* A₂} {σ : B₁ →+* B₂}
  (hcomm : ∀ a, σ (f₁ a) = f₂ (ρ a))
  {M₁ : ModuleCat.{max u₂ v} A₁} {M₂ : ModuleCat.{max u₂ v} A₂}

/-- Base change of a semilinear map along a commuting square of ring homomorphisms, read as a
morphism between the base changes as `ModuleCat` objects. This is the shape the restriction
maps of a presheaf of modules take. -/
noncomputable def squareMapObj (φ : ↑M₁ →ₛₗ[ρ] ↑M₂) :
    (extendScalars f₁).obj M₁ ⟶
      (ModuleCat.restrictScalars σ).obj ((extendScalars f₂).obj M₂) :=
  ModuleCat.semilinearMapAddEquiv σ _ _ (squareMap f₁ f₂ hcomm φ)

/-- `squareMapObj f₁ f₂ hcomm φ` sends `b ⊗ m` to `σ b ⊗ φ m`. -/
@[simp]
lemma ofRestrict_squareMapObj (φ : ↑M₁ →ₛₗ[ρ] ↑M₂) (b : B₁) (m : M₁) :
    ofRestrict σ (squareMapObj f₁ f₂ hcomm φ) (objTmul f₁ b m) = objTmul f₂ (σ b) (φ m) := rfl

end SquareObj

end Stacks.NoncommChangeOfRings

end
